  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-2$ is a natural number\n\nII. $-2$ is an integer\n\nIII. $-2$ is a rational number",
  "worked_solution": "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-2$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-2$ is an integer.\n\n- $-2$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-2)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\"",
